# The TRAINING environment: CPT, fine-tune, eval. No vLLM.
#
# Split from the teacher deliberately. See stack/README.md section 3: a single lockfile
# holding both pulled TWO CUDA wheel families at once (nvidia-cuda-runtime==13.4.92
# alongside nvidia-cuda-runtime-cu12==12.8.90) and installed torch 2.14.0+cu130 while
# claiming to pin 2.11.0+cu128, because vLLM drags in torchaudio/torchvision/torchcodec
# each carrying its own torch constraint.

--extra-index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu128

# torch 2.10, NOT the plan's 2.11, and the reason is causal-conv1d.
#
# causal-conv1d ships prebuilt wheels on its GitHub releases, one per
# (cuda x torch x cxx11abi) combination. At torch 2.11 its setup.py looks for
# `causal_conv1d-1.7.0+cu12torch2.11cxx11abiTRUE-cp312-cp312-linux_x86_64.whl`, which
# **does not exist** in the v1.7.0 release: the published cu12 wheels stop at torch2.10.
# It then falls back to compiling from source with nvcc.
#
# That source build is the single most fragile step in the image. It needs a -devel
# base, it is memory-hungry enough to invite the OOM killer, it compiles for six GPU
# architectures, and it cannot be built under x86 emulation at all -- qemu segfaults
# running nvcc. Pinning one minor version back turns the most fragile step into a wheel
# download.
#
# This is a better engineering choice than the plan's, independent of this Mac: it takes
# a from-source CUDA compile off the critical path of a $1,085 block, where a build
# failure costs $32/hour to debug.
torch==2.10.0

transformers==5.17.0
accelerate
safetensors
datasets
numpy
sentencepiece
einops

# The GDN fast path. CUDA-only; never install these in the Mac environment, because the
# Qwen3.5 modeling file dispatches through a bare importlib.import_module("fla") rather
# than the CUDA-gated helper, and Triton has no Metal backend.
flash-linear-attention
causal-conv1d
